  "synonym_discrimination": null,
  "usage_note": "Unlike **much**, **many** is used freely in ordinary positive statements as well as in questions and negatives: *Many people agree* is natural, while *Much time was spent* sounds more formal than the everyday *A lot of time was spent*. The comparative of **many** is **more**, and the superlative is **most**, shared with **much**. **Many** used alone, without a following noun (*many were invited*), is a separate entry.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "She has many money.",
      "correct": "She has a lot of money.",
      "note": "**Many** goes only with a plural countable noun; an uncountable noun like **money** needs **much** or, in everyday speech, **a lot of**."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "I don't have much friends here.",
      "correct": "I don't have many friends here.",
      "note": "A plural countable noun like **friends** needs **many**, not **much**."
    }
